so just imagine this entire parking lot  pretty much filled with one mirror for a  giant telescope a telescope that will be  the biggest telescope on earth  yeah so it's a it's a it's a little bit  of a fudge because  it's not a symbol that actually gets  used that much it's a circle with a line  through it it's actually more of an  engineering symbol than a physicist  symbol generally speaking in astronomy  bigger is better when it comes to  diameter  all a telescope is is a big light bucket  we just sit out there with a mirror  and collect photons as they come in from  astronomical objects let's take the  story back to the beginning and again as  with most things in astronomy it goes  back to galileo  galileo's first observations with a  telescope he was using a refracting  telescope so a telescope with lenses in  it and the lenses were a few inches  across but basically with optical  telescopes we don't talk about lenses  anymore we talk about mirrors mostly and  the nice thing about mirrors is you can  make them a lot bigger than you can make  lenses so you can build bigger and  bigger telescopes the first telescope  that i ever had some involvement with  was when i was a summer student in  victoria in canada  and that was at the dominion  astrophysical observatory and they have  a lovely telescope there and it's 1.8  meters in diameter  now at the time it was built back in  around 1918 i believe it was for a short  period of time the largest telescope on  earth it was temperamental there  actually was an official stick next to  it with which you had to hit some of the  equipment to make it work and so then we  graduate up into what we might think of  as medium-sized telescopes and these are  telescopes that are maybe  the mirror is maybe four meters in  diameter  so those include telescopes that i've  been to  include the canada france hawaii  telescope in hawaii so of another very  nice summer experience working at that  telescope and something like the william  herschel telescope which is a british  telescope in la palma again a very nice  holiday destination you probably see a  theme  coming in here we tend to build  telescopes where the weather is is good  some of the life of an astronomer is  fairly hard so when you go to hawaii you  may think of beaches and palm trees the  telescopes in hawaii are on top of the  mountain mauna kea they're at 14 000  feet  you're oxygen deprived it looks like the  surface of mars you're above the clouds  you're jet lagged and you're working at  night  so yes you do get to go to the beach at  the end of your observing run  but that's probably after five or six  nights of very very very hard work  well a hubble is kind of the  anomaly here because i said in most  cases bigger is better but the hubble  mirror is only 2.5 meters in diameter  so what's so special about the hubble  well first of all it's in space  so it's not sitting at the bottom of  this really murky moving atmosphere but  not only uh the size of the mirror  matters but the quality of the mirror  and the mirror on hubble was ground to a  precision that i don't think has been  equaled it was incredibly incredibly  precise  of course that's a whole other story  because it was precise in the wrong way  and had to be fixed  thanks to  an accident in the measuring  but it was accurate to i think one  fiftieth of a human hair  so with the hubble it might not be the  biggest telescope on the block but it's  certainly got some of the sharpest  eyesight  yeah so then the next class of  telescopes are something around the six  to eight to ten meter in diameter for  their mirror  so i've used the magellan telescope  in chile so i visited there and stood  underneath that mirror which measures  6.5 meters in diameter which is pretty  big  but we also have lots of telescopes  around the world that have eight meter  mirrors and that's kind of the limit if  you want to build a single lens or a  single mirror you can build a mirror  eight meters across but that's really  pushing the technology because you've  got to build a mirror eight meters  across which is uh optically perfect so  it can actually focus the light so it  has to be figured to within a fraction  of a wavelength of light and it's eight  meters across so from an engineering  point of view it's really pushing the  limits for example if you take a mirror  like that and then you point it in a  different direction which of course is  one of the things you want to do with  the telescope the mirror sags and so you  actually need to have the whole thing  actively controlled with little  pressure pads behind to push it back  into shape so you've got all this sort  of active stuff going on holding it into  shape  so even today the biggest telescopes  which are sort of 10 11 12 meters in  diameter aren't single mirrors we've  gone to this system where you use a  whole series of little segments so you  have lots of hexagonal segments that all  kind of join together like a honeycomb  we have  the amusingly named vlt which is the  very large telescope which is actually  four eight meter telescopes in chile we  the next telescopes the really big ones  are going to be a lot lot bigger than  that  so the one of the first ones that was  bandied about was the the owl the  overwhelmingly large telescope and that  was that was proposed to be a hundred  meters in diameter um so for various  reasons that's probably not going to to  come about so we've downgraded slightly  to instead of the overwhelmingly large  telescope the extremely large telescope  i'm afraid we sort of gave up giving  things exciting names some time ago  it'll probably end up with a more  interesting name when it actually gets  built but for the moment that's what  it's known as  and that's going to have a mirror that  will be 42 meters in diameter so it's a  whole series of more than a thousand  individual segments that are all going  to be joined together to make a single  mirror effectively that's 42 meters in  diameter which as you might imagine is  going to collect an absolute shed load  of light so we're going to be seeing  able to see things which we stand no  chance at all of seeing at the moment  we'll be able to see planets like the  earth orbiting around other nearby stars  and even be able to study the  atmospheres of those planets to figure  out whether there's life on them we'll  be able to see stars in other galaxies  where at the moment we can just see kind  of the fuzzy blur of light we're going  to be able to see galaxies on the other  side of the universe on the kind of  accuracy where currently we can only see  nearby galaxies so it's going to  completely revolutionize astronomy  42 meters 42 meters should we should we  try and get an idea how big that is what  are you going to do what are you gonna  do um so one easy measurement i have is  the computer that goes on my bike so  we're going to go outside and i'm going  to get on my bike and see if we can  measure approximately 42 meters  somewhere in the parking lot and that's  the sad thing these poor photons of  light they've been traveling across the  gulf of space for you know 10 billion  years  hoping some astronomers going to catch  them and they you know they run splat  into the roof of your house and that's  it it's all over so we'll catch some of  them i mean even with a mirror 42 meters  in diameter we're still not going to  catch very many of them but it's a much  bigger bucket than we've had in the past  so we're going to collect a lot more  light than we ever have before okay so  i've got my measuring instrument here  which is my bike and i have a little  wireless bike computer so that just sits  on here so i'm going to reset that now  and we're going to try to see if we can  figure out just how big 42 meters  actually is where they're going to put  this bad boy  that's still it's an interesting  question because obviously there are  kind of astronomical reasons as to why  you might want to put in particular  places for example there's some things  you can only see from the southern  hemisphere there's some things you can  only see from the northern hemisphere so  that's one issue  um you want to put it somewhere dark  because you want to you know you don't  want light pollution to mess up your  images but equally actually these things  consume rather a large amount of power  so at their peak they can use as much  power as a small town so you need  somewhere where there's kind of the  infrastructure you can get electricity  to them you have road access and all  that kind of stuff so you can't really  pick somewhere that's absolutely in the  middle of nowhere  and finally there's politics you know  you've got to put it somewhere that's  politically stable you probably don't  want to put it somewhere where there's  going to be a revolution someone's going  to nationalize your telescope 10 minutes  after you've built it  and even you know if you're trying to  get money out of someone it's usually  easier to get money if you say and i'm  going to build it in your country so  there's lots and lots of things and it's  still i think it will be finalized for  the eelt sometime later this year but  it's still open for discussion at the  moment  okay so this distance is 42 meters that  i've just traveled and that's going to  be the diameter of the mirror of the  extremely large telescope that's going  to be built  so just imagine this entire parking lot  pretty much filled with one mirror for a  giant telescope a telescope that will be  the biggest telescope on earth
 

